---
import Post from '../../layouts/Post.astro';
import { getPost } from '../../data/posts';

const post = getPost('signs-your-water-heater-is-failing');

const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the first sign a water heater is going bad?',
    a: 'Usually sound. Popping or rumbling when the burner fires means sediment has built up on the bottom of the tank, and that is the condition that drives most later failures. It normally shows up long before you notice anything wrong with the hot water itself.' },
  { q: 'Is rusty hot water always the water heater?',
    a: 'Not always, but it is the most likely cause if only the hot side is discolored and it clears on the cold. If both hot and cold run rusty, suspect the supply or galvanised pipework in the house instead.' },
  { q: 'Should I worry about a small puddle under my water heater?',
    a: 'Yes, enough to find out where it is coming from. Condensation and a weeping relief valve are both harmless-ish and fixable. Water seeping from the base of the tank itself means internal corrosion, and that unit needs replacing before it lets go.' },
  { q: 'How quickly does a failing water heater become an emergency?',
    a: 'A tank can go from a slow seep to a full failure in anything from weeks to minutes — there is no reliable way to predict it. That is why pooling water is the one symptom we treat as urgent rather than something to monitor.' },
  { q: 'Can a failing water heater raise my gas bill?',
    a: 'It can. Sediment insulates the bottom of the tank from the burner, so the burner runs longer to reach the same temperature. A creeping bill with no change in household habits is worth investigating.' },
];
---
<Post
  image="/images/water-heater-leak-repair-rocklin-old-tank-pipework.webp"
  imageAlt="Water heater pipework and valves showing the early signs of a failing unit"
  imageW={1600}
  imageH={2400}
  imageCaption="Rust, noise and lukewarm water usually show up long before a tank actually fails."
  title={post.title}
  description="Rusty water, popping noises, lukewarm showers, pooling water and a weeping relief valve — what each warning sign means and how urgent it really is."
  path={`/blog/${post.slug}`}
  published={post.published}
  excerpt={post.excerpt}
  faqs={faqs}
  ctaHeading="Spotted One of These on Your Own Tank?"
  ctaText="Tell us the symptom on the phone and we will tell you whether it is urgent, routine, or something you can safely keep an eye on."
>
  <p>
    Water heaters almost always warn you before they fail. The problem is that the warnings are
    quiet, gradual, and easy to normalise &mdash; you stop hearing the popping, you get used to
    the shorter shower, you decide the damp patch on the slab has always been there. Then the
    tank lets go on a weekend.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here are the signals worth paying attention to, in rough order of how seriously we treat them
    when a Rocklin homeowner describes them over the phone.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Rusty or Discolored Hot Water</h2>
  <p>
    Run the hot tap into a white cup. If the water looks tea-colored, brown or has visible
    flecks, something metallic is breaking down. Now run the cold tap. If the cold is clear, the
    heater is the source; if both are discolored, the problem is upstream in the supply or in old
    galvanised pipework.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rust on the hot side usually means the anode rod has been consumed and the glass lining of
    the tank is no longer fully protected. That is not instantly fatal &mdash; on a younger tank,
    replacing the anode rod and flushing can genuinely extend its life. On a tank already past ten
    years, discolored hot water is a strong hint that the steel is corroding and the countdown
    has started.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Popping, Rumbling or Crackling</h2>
  <p>
    Sediment is the single most common cause of noise, and noise is the earliest warning most
    people get. Minerals drop out of the water and settle on the bottom of the tank, directly
    above the gas burner. Water trapped underneath that layer superheats and forces its way
    through it, which is the popping you hear.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why it matters: that sediment blanket insulates the burner from the water it is supposed to be
    heating, so the burner runs longer and the steel at the bottom of the tank runs hotter than it
    was designed to. Overheated steel means stressed glass lining, and stressed lining is where
    corrosion begins. Caught early, this is a flush, not a replacement.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Lukewarm Water, or Hot Water That Runs Out Fast</h2>
  <p>
    If showers start warm and go cold faster than they used to, you are losing usable capacity.
    The usual culprits:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sediment</strong> taking up volume at the bottom of the tank and blocking heat transfer.</li>
    <li><strong>A failed lower heating element</strong> on an electric unit &mdash; the classic symptom is a small amount of hot water followed by cold.</li>
    <li><strong>A broken dip tube</strong>, which lets incoming cold water mix straight into the hot outlet instead of being delivered to the bottom.</li>
    <li><strong>A thermostat drifting out of calibration</strong>, so the unit never reaches its set point.</li>
    <li><strong>A unit that was always undersized</strong> for the household, which has nothing to do with failure at all.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Water that is lukewarm everywhere, all the time, is different from water that starts hot and
    runs out. Tell your plumber which one you have &mdash; it narrows the diagnosis immediately.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Water Pooling Under the Tank</h2>
  <p>
    This is the one symptom we treat as urgent. Before you panic, check the three innocent
    explanations:
  </p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Condensation</strong> On a humid day, or on a newly refilled tank, cold lines and the tank base can sweat. It dries up and does not return.</li>
    <li><strong>The relief valve discharge</strong> Follow the pipe from the T&amp;P valve. If the water is coming out of the end of that line, the valve is discharging &mdash; a real issue, but not a dead tank.</li>
    <li><strong>Fittings and the drain valve</strong> Dry everything, then look again in an hour. A drip from a union, a nipple or the drain valve is a repair.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    If none of those explain it and water is appearing at the base of the tank itself, the inner
    tank has corroded through. There is no repair for that. Shut the cold inlet valve on top of
    the heater, turn the gas control to off or switch off the breaker, and arrange a
    <a href="/water-heater-replacement-rocklin">replacement</a> rather than waiting to see what
    happens.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Age</h2>
  <p>
    Age is not a symptom, but it is the context that makes every other symptom more or less
    serious. A conventional tank in a hard-water area typically gives somewhere in the region of
    ten to thirteen years &mdash; a general industry range, not a guarantee in either direction.
    Find the manufacture date on the data plate, or use the serial number, and write it on the
    jacket in marker. Then the next time something odd happens you are not guessing.
  </p>
  <p>
    At eight years, a strange noise is a maintenance job. At fourteen, the same noise is a
    budgeting conversation.
  </p>

  <h2>6. A Gas or Power Bill That Keeps Creeping Up</h2>
  <p>
    Water heating is one of the larger energy loads in a typical home, so a heater losing
    efficiency shows up on the bill. The mechanism is the same sediment story: the burner has to
    fire longer to push heat through a mineral layer, and a tank with degraded insulation loses
    more standby heat between draws.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rule out the obvious first &mdash; a new occupant, a longer-shower teenager, a seasonal rate
    change, a leaking hot tap or a failed recirculation pump timer running constantly. If none of
    those fit and the trend is steady over several bills, the heater is worth a look.
  </p>

  <h2>7. A Temperature &amp; Pressure Relief Valve That Weeps</h2>
  <p>
    The T&amp;P valve is the safety device that stops a water heater becoming dangerous. If it is
    dripping or discharging, it is either faulty or it is responding to a genuine over-pressure or
    over-temperature condition. Both need attention; neither should be plugged, capped or ignored.
  </p>
  <p>
    Common causes are a thermostat set too high, a failed thermal expansion tank, high incoming
    water pressure, or simply an old valve that no longer seats. Testing the valve, checking static
    pressure at a hose bib and confirming the expansion tank still holds a charge sorts out which
    one you have.
  </p>

  <h2>What to Do With All of This</h2>
  <p>
    Group the signs into two piles. Noise, slow recovery, mild discoloration and a creeping bill
    are maintenance-and-diagnosis territory &mdash; they often come back from a flush, an anode
    rod and a <a href="/">repair</a>. Pooling water at the base of the tank, rust bleeding through
    the jacket, or any of the above on a unit well past twelve years old belong in the
    <a href="/water-heater-replacement-rocklin">replacement</a> pile.
  </p>
  <p>
    The genuinely useful habit is to look at your heater once a year. Two minutes with a
    flashlight &mdash; checking for moisture, rust streaks, scorching near the burner and the
    condition of the relief discharge line &mdash; catches almost everything on this list while it
    is still cheap.
  </p>
</Post>
